[Handbook: Seborrheic Keratoses and other Benign Tumors]
Also known as seborrhoeic keratosis or basal cell papilloma.  Also known as seborrhoeic keratosis or basal cell papilloma.  Benign epidermal tumours of unknown cause. Common in the elderly. They are commonest on the trunk but also affect the face and limbs. Patients may have one, several, or hundreds. Treatment is only required if the seborrhoeic wart is traumatized, when it may become inflamed or bleed, sometimes simulating a melanoma.  Benign epidermal tumours of unknown cause. Common in the elderly. They are commonest on the trunk but also affect the face and limbs. Patients may have one, several, or hundreds. Treatment is only required if the seborrhoeic wart is traumatized, when it may become inflamed or bleed, sometimes simulating a melanoma.  What should I look for?  What should I look for?  · A well-defined oval, pigmented tumour, around $0.5{-}2mathsf{c m}$ in diameter, that appears stuck onto the surface of the skin. •    Rough surface that may feel slightly greasy. Dark pits studded over the surface—-easier to see with magnification using dermoscopy. ·Colour can range from a pale pink-brown to dark brown or black. •  Seborrhoeic warts may be virtually flat or raised by several mm.  · A well-defined oval, pigmented tumour, around $0.5{-}2\mathsf{c m}$ in diameter, that appears stuck onto the surface of the skin. •    Rough surface that may feel slightly greasy. Dark pits studded over the surface—-easier to see with magnification using dermoscopy. ·Colour can range from a pale pink-brown to dark brown or black. •  Seborrhoeic warts may be virtually flat or raised by several mm.
